g": "Six Week Summer", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This course provides an introduction to the science of the mind.\u00a0 Everyone knows what it's like to think and perceive, but this subjective experience provides little insight into how minds emerge from physical intities like brains.\u00a0 To address this issue, cognitive science integrates work from at least five disciplines: Psychology, Neuroscience, Computer Science, Linguistics, and Philosophy.\u00a0 This course introduces students to the insights these disciplines offer into the workings of the mind by exploring visual perception, attention, memory, learning, problem solving, language, and consciousness.\u00a0", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(KCM-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring, Summer (six-week session).", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2734, "crseId": 351496, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "COGST", "catalogNbr": "1111", "titleShort": "Making a Difference by Design", "titleLong": "Making a Difference by Design", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 1060, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "09:30AM", "timeEnd": "12:00PM", "startDt": "07/15/2019", "endDt": "08/02/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 5, "netid": "ne237", "firstName": "Nooshin", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Ahmadi"}, {"instrAssignSeq": 4, "netid": "rbh25", "firstName": "Brooke", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Hollis"}, {"instrAssignSeq": 6, "netid": "haf3", "firstName": "Heather", "middleName": "A.", "lastName": "Kolakowski"}], "pattern": "MTWRF", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 217", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS217", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "07/15/2019", "endDt": "08/02/2019", "addConsent": "D", "addConsentDescr": "Department Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "DEA", "catalogNbr": "1110", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "SP2", "sessionBeginDt": "05/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "08/06/2019", "sessionLong": "Summer Special Session 2", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "Making a Difference by Design is a course about leadership, creative problem solving and social change. Design is not just about products, it is about strategy, communication, empowerment, growth and social change, and has a specific impact on health and hospitality. DEA 1110 was conceived as a companion to DEA 1100, Design Generation(s), although it is not necessary to take both classes.\u00a0 The course is not a class about becoming a designer. It presumes no pre-requisites or artistic abilities.\u00a0 It is an introductory level course for non-designers and designers alike which examines how design affects you personally and globally, and how you can affect design process to innovate in various fields.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall, Summer.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "Lab fee: $15.", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Demonstrates a basic understanding of creative design process - information gathering, idea exploration, iterative feedback loops, prototyping and testing - and an awareness of key factors affecting creativity and teamwork (innovate in research, design or practice).", "Recognize design as both a leadership and strategic business planning tool with both tangible and intangible outcomes and impacts across a variety of stakeholders (apply multi-disciplinary perspective).", "Understand the relationship between health, hospitality and design."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2734, "crseId": 353568, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "COGST", "catalogNbr": "1500", "titleShort": "Intro to Environ. Psychology", "titleLong": "Introduction to Environmental Psychology", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 1105, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:00AM", "timeEnd": "11:15AM", "startDt": "06/24/2019", "endDt": "08/02/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 4, "netid": "ksp66", "firstName": "Kathryn", "middleName": "Sandra", "lastName": "Peditto"}], "pattern": "MTWRF", "facilityDescr": "Uris Hall 262", "bldgDescr": "Uris Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "URH262", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "06/24/2019", "endDt": "08/02/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "DEA", "catalogNbr": "1500", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "PSYCH", "catalogNbr": "1500", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "6WK", "sessionBeginDt": "06/24/2019", "sessionEndDt": "08/02/2019", "sessionLong": "Six Week Summer", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "Environmental Psychology is an interdisciplinary field concerned with how the physical environment and human behavior interrelate. Most of the course focuses on how residential environments and urban and natural settings affect human health and well-being. Students also examine how human attitudes and behaviors affect environmental quality. Issues of environmental justice and culture are included throughout. Hands-on projects plus exams. Lecture and discussion sections.\u00a0DEA 1501\u00a0 - Writing in the major (WIM) option also is available (by instructor permission) for 4 credits.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(SBA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring, Summer.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Provide overview of knowledge about the environment and human behavior (grounding in field)", "Understand cultural and life course diversity in human-environment interactions (sensitivity to diversity)", "Learn how to analyze problems like an environmental psychologist (develop critical thinking skill)"], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": n